    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it’s just hard to believe in \"treatment\" when the soul of the thing is gone. it’s like being told to fix a book after all the pages have been ripped out. if the purpose is gone, isn't the rest just... unnecessary? it feels like i'm just occupying space that doesn't belong to me anymore. like a ghost that hasn't realized it's dead yet.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is incredibly painful to feel like the very essence of your self has been lost, and that metaphor of the ripped-out book is powerful. When the purpose feels gone, the act of \"rebuilding\" can seem absurd or impossible. Instead of trying to \"fix the soul,\" consider if the immediate need is to accept the *emptiness* for a while, without pressure to fill it. Sometimes, the first act of self-compassion is to let the \"unnecessary\" feeling exist without self-criticism.",
      "source": "target",
